\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Main components of a simplified ECG beat. Shape features describe the P--QRS--T pattern, while RR features describe timing between beats. This is an educational drawing, not a diagnostic template.}}{1}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Real four-second ECG examples from the MIT--BIH Arrhythmia Database: normal record 100, PVC record 208, PAC record 232, LBBB record 109, RBBB record 118, and AFib record 203. The dashed line marks the annotated central R peak. The examples are used for explanation and are not clinical diagnostic templates.}}{3}{figure.caption.11}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Dataset roles and evaluation boundaries. The healthy NSRDB channel creates the frozen normal model. MITDB MLII creates the supervised feature table and is split chronologically into development and temporal-test regions.}}{13}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces MLII polarity and the channel used by each model.}}{14}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Cleaning real ECG for the two roles. The normal-reference example is the first four seconds of NSRDB 16272/ECG1, with its actual archived scaling. The supervised example is an MLII beat from MITDB 100, filtered and standardised within its own window. Both become one 512-sample input; their channel identities remain different.}}{18}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Preparation of normal-reference windows and labelled MLII beats.}}{21}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Two evidence scales used by the final model. Morphology describes the local shape of a beat; RR timing describes rhythm context around the beat.}}{22}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.6}{\ignorespaces Learning map for the final ECG system.}}{23}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.7}{\ignorespaces Feature-extraction operations behind the final 242-value vector. The tree models learn from compact numerical evidence, not from raw ECG samples directly.}}{24}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.8}{\ignorespaces Normal-only reconstruction principle. A larger residual can indicate novelty, but it is evidence only; the final arrhythmia decision is made by the supervised hierarchy.}}{27}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.9}{\ignorespaces Autoencoder and Extra Trees training mechanisms.}}{29}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.10}{\ignorespaces Probability logic of the final hierarchy. The subtype model is conditional: it is used only after the abnormality gate is passed.}}{30}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.11}{\ignorespaces Theoretical reason for subject-clustered validation. Clustered resampling prevents one long record from being treated as many independent people.}}{33}{figure.caption.37}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.12}{\ignorespaces Exact runtime order of the final ECG system. The autoencoder supplies features; the supervised tree models make the final class decision.}}{35}{figure.caption.39}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.13}{\ignorespaces R-peak-aligned beat window used by the final model. The R peak is at sample index 200, giving the model pre-QRS context and post-QRS recovery information.}}{36}{figure.caption.40}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.14}{\ignorespaces Training and use of the saved three-component model.}}{38}{figure.caption.42}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.15}{\ignorespaces Autoencoder architecture with verified tensor dimensions.}}{39}{figure.caption.45}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.16}{\ignorespaces Autoencoder fitting and validation loss.}}{42}{figure.caption.50}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.17}{\ignorespaces Real healthy and abnormal ECG examples through the frozen autoencoder. The residual between input and reconstruction is converted into four numerical features for the supervised detector.}}{43}{figure.caption.52}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.18}{\ignorespaces Feature-vector layout used by both supervised models. The same 242-value vector is passed first to the abnormality detector and, if required, to the subtype classifier.}}{44}{figure.caption.53}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.19}{\ignorespaces Worked example of entropy reduction in a tree.}}{46}{figure.caption.57}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.20}{\ignorespaces Real fitting and temporal-test ECG segments.}}{62}{figure.caption.64}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.21}{\ignorespaces Real ECG through one training update and the saved autoencoder.}}{64}{figure.caption.65}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.22}{\ignorespaces Real test PVC from source ECG to subtype scores.}}{67}{figure.caption.67}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.23}{\ignorespaces Real temporal-test examples of all six outputs.}}{72}{figure.caption.69}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.24}{\ignorespaces Chronological model selection, refitting, and final testing.}}{74}{figure.caption.71}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.25}{\ignorespaces Temporal split audit for every eligible MITDB MLII record. Blue beats are available for final fitting, yellow beats are the boundary gap before the test, and red beats are the held-out final 20\% temporal test.}}{76}{figure.caption.72}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.26}{\ignorespaces Model selection on the 64--80\% validation region. The binary detector is selected by balanced accuracy and F1; the subtype classifier is selected by macro F1 and accuracy.}}{77}{figure.caption.73}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.27}{\ignorespaces Populations used for the three evaluation questions.}}{78}{figure.caption.74}%
